   "title": "Your Villages Weekend Recap: Rock at Brownwood, Goodbye Fenney Grill",
   "date": "July 20, 2026 · By Susan Whitfield, Villages insider",
   "body": "<p>Morning, neighbors. Grab your coffee &mdash; here's the scoop on the weekend we just wrapped up.</p>\n\n<p>It was a quieter July weekend by Villages standards. Hot, a little stormy in the afternoons, and the squares carried the show. But we did lose an old friend on the dining side. More on that in a minute.</p>\n\n<h3>Brownwood had the beat all weekend</h3>\n<p>Brownwood Paddock Square ran the table Friday through Sunday, per <a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/brownwood/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">the official Brownwood entertainment calendar</a>.</p>\n<p>Friday night belonged to <strong>Rocky and The Rollers</strong>. Twenty-seven years playing The Villages, and they still pack the square. If you were there, you know. The dance floor filled before the first song ended.</p>\n<p>Saturday brought <strong>The Voodoo Gypsies</strong>, the classic rock crew out of Central Florida. Rock 'n roll hits from the '60s, '70s, and '80s, exactly the way we like them.</p>\n<p>Sunday wound things down with <strong>Daisy Fever</strong>, taking us from Woodstock to Studio 54. Folk, soul, disco, and classic rock. A nice mellow-to-groovy finish for the weekend.</p>\n<p>Quick reminder while we're at it: through September 30, square music runs <strong>6 to 10 p.m.</strong> instead of the usual 5 to 9. That's the <a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/entertainment-summer-hours/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">official summer-hours shift</a> to dodge the worst of the heat and the afternoon storms. Plan your golf cart ride accordingly.</p>\n\n<h3>We said goodbye to the Fenney Grill</h3>\n<p>Here's the bittersweet one. The Fenney Grill served its last meals Sunday, July 19, according to <a href=\"https://www.villages-news.com/2026/07/18/restaurant-in-the-villages-will-be-closing-its-doors/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Villages-News.com</a>.</p>\n<p>If you live down south, you remember what that place meant. It opened in 2017 as the first restaurant in the Fenney area, back when that end of The Villages felt like the frontier. Plenty of us had our first meal south of 44 right there on that porch.</p>\n<p>The writing had been on the wall &mdash; they'd cut back to summer hours in May, and it's been a bumpy stretch since. I won't pile on. I'll just say thanks for nine years of burgers with a view, and I'll keep my ears open for what moves into that spot. You'll hear it here first.</p>\n\n<h3>One for the road</h3>\n<p>If you're headed to a square this week, the bar huts are pouring their July special, the red-white-and-blue <strong>Freedom Punch</strong>, $8 for a single, while supplies last. Details on the <a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/bar-huts/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">bar hut page</a>. It's as patriotic as a drink gets, and it goes down easy on a 90-degree evening. Trust me on this one.</p>\n\n<p>That's the weekend, friends. A little music, a little farewell, a lot of humidity. This week's lineup is already posted on <a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/nightly-entertainment/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">the entertainment calendars</a> &mdash; Paradise plays Brownwood tonight and Rocky and The Rollers slide over to Lake Sumter Landing.</p>\n\n<p>See you around the square,<br>&mdash; Susan</p>"
  },
