   "title": "Your Villages Weekend Recap: Music, Bingo & a Cold One",
   "date": "June 22, 2026 · By Susan Whitfield, Villages insider",
   "body": "<p>Well, friends, we made it through another hot one. Here's the scoop on the weekend that just wrapped, and trust me, the squares did not disappoint.</p><p>Friday night I had a choice to make, and so did you. Every square had a band going at 6:00. Over at <a href=\"https://www.villages-news.com/2026/06/18/live-square-entertainment-for-friday-june-19/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Brownwood Paddock Square</a>, it was <strong>Eighteen87</strong> cranking out the hits. Lake Sumter Landing had <strong>Blue Stone Circle</strong>, the ultimate party band, rock to reggae to Top 40. Spanish Springs pulled out the big guns with <strong>Rocky and The Rollers</strong>, a Villages favorite for 27 years now. Sawgrass Grove had <strong>Zee-R Band</strong>, and Eastport's main stage gave us <strong>The Edge Of Rock</strong>. Five squares, five bands, one happy town.</p><p>I parked my cart at Lake Sumter and stayed put. No regrets.</p><h3>Saturday &amp; Sunday on the squares</h3><p>Saturday I bounced back to the lake for <a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/lake-sumter-landing/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Voodoo Gypsies</a>, a classic rock cover band out of Central Florida. They play the good stuff, '60s through '80s, the songs you already know every word to. If you wanted to dance instead, <a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/brownwood/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Brownwood went full club mode</a> with CLUB CTRL featuring DJ Lu-S. Lights, beats, the works.</p><p>Sunday wound things down sweet. Lake Sumter brought back <a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/lake-sumter-landing/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">September Dogs</a>, that female-fronted four-piece with the big layered vocals. And Brownwood closed the weekend with <a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/brownwood/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sheila &amp; The Sound</a>, running everything from Aretha to Journey. By the last song, half the square was singing along.</p><h3>Beat the heat</h3><p>Here's a little something worth knowing if you've been melting out there. The squares moved their summer hours. Through August 31, the bands now play <a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/lake-sumter-landing/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">6:00 to 10:00 PM instead of 5:00 to 9:00</a>. Happy hour stays put at 5:00. Smart move, honestly. So if you've been skipping nights because the afternoon felt like a furnace, come a little later. The good stuff starts at six now.</p><p>And speaking of beating the heat, the bar huts are pouring a <strong>Tequila Sunrise</strong> this June while supplies last. Eight bucks for a single, ten-fifty for a double. I had one Saturday between sets. It did the job. You're welcome. (<a href=\"https://www.thevillagesentertainment.com/bar-huts/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">bar huts</a>)</p><h3>Who-knew note</h3><p>For my catalogue folks always asking what to <em>do</em> around here. The POA has <a href=\"https://www.villages-news.com/2026/06/18/poa-will-host-bingo-night-at-everglades-recreation-center/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">bingo night coming up at the Everglades Recreation Center</a>. It's an easy, friendly way to meet a few neighbors if you're new in town. I've sent more than one new resident their way, and they always come back grinning. Tell 'em Susan sent you.</p><p>One more thing that made me smile. Villages-News ran a sweet piece on a couple here who <a href=\"https://www.villages-news.com/2026/06/15/cant-believe-weve-been-married-70-years/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">just hit 70 years of marriage</a>. Seventy! That's the kind of story that reminds me why I love this place.</p><h3>The bottom line</h3><p>I'll be straight with you, since I always am. It was a quieter weekend on the news front. No big festival, no grand opening I could confirm. So this week the squares carried the show, and that's just fine by me. Some weekends, good music and a cold drink are the whole point.</p><p>If you missed it all, don't fret. The bands roll on every single night, six to ten, all summer long. Pick a square, grab a seat, and let somebody else do the singing.</p><p>See you around the square,<br>— Susan</p>"
  },
